A||||10||Gokart||V8 Archie?
Z||000000||Gokart||07-21-99||07:49 PM||||I'm thinking about saving some dough and putting a V8 in my GT. Do I really need all the stuff that V8 Archie sells to install one? I was told that all I need is to change my engine mount! Sounds too easy! My Pontiac dealer wants 3000.00 WITH installation for a V6 and 3 years or 36,000 mile warranty, maybe I should go that route? Any suggestions!?!
Z||000001||Phil||07-21-99||08:58 PM||||I have not done a v8 conversion yet but in my experience I have found that when you make a dramatic change such as a v8 into a Fiero you will most likely need everything that they sell in the kit plus the infamous "locally obtained parts" Also be safe and figure about another 25% in costs and double their estimated installation time. Then about 2 weeks of trouble shooting the little things. Other than that no problem.
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||07-21-99||09:37 PM||||I just took the 4 cylinder out of my 88 and installed a V6. The weight difference is VERY noticeable. I don't think I would ever put a V8 in my Fiero. If you like to go fast in a straight line, then it may be what you need,but forget about handeling....right? batboy, you have an answer to how it handles?
Z||000003||batboy||07-21-99||09:47 PM||||Gokart, I have a V8 Fiero, although the previous owner actually did the swap, I can answer some of your questions. Whoever told you that all you needed to do was motor mounts was obviously hallucinating. There is no way you can bolt the Chevy small block V8 onto the Fiero tranny without a special adapter plate. You also have to cut a hole into the right rear fender well for the water pump pulley and run a special belt setup outside the engine compartment to a special crank pulley inside the wheel well next to the rear tire. You have use a remote oil filter. You need special lower engine mounts to secure it to the cradle and a special mount on top for a dogbone engine mount. You have to relocate the battery. Since you're adding up to a 150 pounds of extra weight, you need new heavier rear springs. Changing the wiring can be a nightmare. The water pump mounts upside down and coolant hoses must be rerouted. You want A/C? Another nightmare. The exhaust system requires lots of work and fabrication too. The list goes on and on, but I think you get the picture. If you are really handy at modifying and engineering stuff, you might get away with just buying the basic kit and doing the rest yourself. The average person (such as myself) probably ought to get the complete master conversion kit. Most people I've talked to say to budget at least $3,000 for the swap, and that's not counting the engine. Phil is right about everything except the two weeks of troubleshooting. Better make that two months. When you're done, you have a play toy not a everyday driver, that's why I also have a second Fiero with a 4 cylinder as my commuter car. Hope this gives you an idea what you're up against.
Z||000004||batboy||07-21-99||09:53 PM||||Yes, handling does suffer. There are ways to get it to handle better (not great mind you), but that involves much more expense and work too. Good luck.
Z||000005||lowCG||07-22-99||01:01 AM||||Yeah,the V8 Vega swap was vever  as easy as it was meant to sound either.<br>SCCA,you've got me scared now;just what is the weight difference between the 4,and 6cyl.engines?
Z||000006||Gokart||07-22-99||04:36 AM||||I'll sticking with a V6! Thanks! I was just thinking it would be nice to "upgrade" my Fiero some how in the future. Maybe a turbo engine installed or a street chip. I'll forget about V8's!
Z||000007||PaUL||07-22-99||02:46 PM||||You could get a larger V6, the 3.1 and 3.4s are pretty much the same as the 2.8 externally. Another V6 you could install is the Buick 3.8 but this is a completely different design. However it does have the same transmission bolt pattern as the 2.8 and would not require an adapter plate. <p>I dont think the difference in weight between v6 and l4 engines is that great. I certainly didnt notice it when I swapped out my 2.5 L4 with the 2.8 V6. In fact I found the bare 2.8 block to be amazingly light. I could pick it up with one hand. 
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||07-22-99||03:53 PM||||Have you ever autocrossed a 4 cylinder Fiero and a V6 Fiero? That is when the weight(and power) is noticeable. When I had the 4 cylinder and the V6 on the ground, we could lift the 4 cylinder, but not the V6. I am not sure how much each one weights, but when you put 100 pounds in the passenger compartment and 100 pounds in the trunk......<p>
Z||000009||PaUL||07-22-99||04:02 PM||||I probably didnt notice any difference in weight because my 2.8 is basic with no AC compressor, fuel injection, cruise control, or any kind of emissions control devices. All those parts probably adds up to about 100lbs.
Z||000010||lowCG||07-23-99||02:00 AM||||I'm going to be putting my suspension components,chosen from when I had a 4cyl car,no AC, to work in my new car,a V6,w/AC.I hope a small rear swaybar will put everything back in balance again.Might have to bump up a notch in the spring stiffness if that doesn't do it.
